::CW_U02_L08_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Passage: "The elevator stopped between floors. Jalen laughed once, too loudly, then pressed the emergency button with the same finger he used to hide the tremble in his hand." Which skill is most clearly being assessed?{
~A. Formatting a poem into lines.#Incorrect. The passage is prose and focuses on narrative craft, not poetic line breaks.
=B. Revealing character through action and controlled detail.#Correct. Jalen's laugh, button press, and hidden tremble show fear without simply announcing it.
~C. Listing unrelated setting details.#Incorrect. The elevator matters because it creates pressure, not because the writer is listing objects.
~D. Explaining the full history of elevators.#Incorrect. The passage uses setting to create scene tension, not to provide informational history.
}

::CW_U02_L08_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Passage: "I told myself the lake looked smaller than it had when I was ten, but I still stepped around the dock's broken board." What does the first-person point of view help the reader understand?{
=A. The narrator's private attempt to seem braver than they feel.#Correct. First person gives direct access to the narrator's self-talk and hesitation.
~B. The exact measurements of the lake.#Incorrect. The line says the lake "looked smaller," but the focus is perception and memory, not measurement.
~C. Every other character's thoughts.#Incorrect. First person usually limits the reader to one narrator's experience.
~D. A complete map of the dock.#Incorrect. The broken board is a meaningful detail, but the passage does not map the whole dock.
}

::CW_U02_L08_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.FIC.01] Passage: Draft purpose: show that a character is avoiding a difficult truth. Which detail best supports that purpose?{
~A. The character names every item on a grocery receipt.#Incorrect. A list may be realistic, but it does not automatically show avoidance.
~B. The character wears blue shoes.#Incorrect. This could matter only if the scene connects the shoes to the conflict, which the option does not do.
=C. The character changes the subject each time the unopened letter is mentioned.#Correct. Repeatedly shifting away from the letter directly reveals avoidance through action.
~D. The character walks down a street at noon.#Incorrect. This sets time and place but does not show the difficult truth being avoided.
}

::CW_U02_L08_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.FIC.03] Passage: "The storm had passed, but water still dripped from the ceiling into the only clean bowl. Lila moved the bowl under the leak, then set her birthday candles beside it." Which interpretation best connects setting and meaning?{
~A. The scene is only about weather.#Incorrect. The storm matters, but the birthday candles make the moment about more than weather.
~B. The bowl has no purpose in the scene.#Incorrect. The bowl catches water and helps show the character managing hardship.
~C. The candles prove the setting is a bakery.#Incorrect. Candles can appear in many settings; the passage points to a home under stress.
=D. The wet ceiling and candles show celebration mixed with hardship.#Correct. The details work together: damage remains, but Lila is still trying to mark a birthday.
}

::CW_U02_L08_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.FIC.05] Passage: A student revises a scene by cutting three paragraphs of backstory and adding one line of dialogue at the moment the character must choose. What is the most likely reason this improves the scene?{
~A. Scenes should never include background information.#Incorrect. Background can be useful, but too much can delay the central moment.
=B. The revision makes the turning point more immediate and focused.#Correct. Cutting excess backstory and placing dialogue at the choice can make the reader experience the decision more directly.
~C. Dialogue is always better than description.#Incorrect. Dialogue is one tool. It helps only when it reveals character, conflict, or decision.
~D. The revision removes all narrative technique.#Incorrect. Focused dialogue is itself a narrative technique when used purposefully.
}
